e a  three left over so you can see we're  getting 2 times 2 which is four square  root of three okay so same result and  then this example here we don't want to  have a radical in the denominator that's  considered improper see what you want to  do is you want to rationalize and way  you do that is you x square root of 2  over square root of two because square  root of two times square root of two  thats square root of four which is two  and that's getting rid of the radical in  the denominator but whatever you do the  desire you want to do to the numerator  as well so then here we're multiplying a  number times a radical we just get a  number around  we've got it now there's one other  scenario and that's it if you have  something like this where it's a  binomial okay you have two terms in the  denominator and you still have that  radical you want to get rid of that  radical what you're going to do is  you're going to multiply by the  conjugate and what the conjugate is it's  the same quantity you have here you're  just changing that sign in between the  two values okay so this was + this would  be minus you don't want to change this  from 1 to negative 1 just to sign in  between the two terms okay so that what  you want to do is you just want to  multiply this out so let me see if I can  do that for you here so 3 times 1 and 3  times root 2 so x 3 plus 3 root 2 and in  the denominator we're going to do is I'm  gonna do the foil or you can distribute  twice so 1 times 1 is 1 1 times root 2  is positive root 2 negative root 2 times  1 is negative root 2 and then negative  root 2 times positive 2 is negative  square root of four but that's actually  going to be square root of 4 is 2 so  that's going to be negative to the root  2 and a route to cancel one minus two is  negative one so we get 3 plus 3 root to  all over negative 1 but you could divide  both these by negative 1 and just make  it negative 3 minus 3 root 2 okay so  that's just a general overview of how to  work with radicals let's do an example  here so you can do this on your own  pause the video it says even if y equals  3 square root of three and five times y  equals the square root of three X what  is the value of x right so go ahead and  pause the video see if you can do that  in your own and now we'll go through it  together so what I would do is this y  equals 3 root 3 I would put that in  place the wire right there so it's just  a substitution right so we have 5 times  3 square root of 3 equals the square  root of 3x now remember when you  multiply number times number right so 5  times 3 is 15 square root of 3 or I'm  not multiplying the five times the  square root of three just the two  integers together right okay Matty  equals the square root of three X our  goal is to get X by itself so what's the  opposite of taking the square root logs  to square both sides I'm going to square  both sides like this the square the  square root cancel you get 3x here 15  root 3-squared remember that's really  like 15 root 3 times another 15 root 3  right because you're squaring it you  have two of them 15 x 15 is 225 square  root 3 times square root of 3 is square  root of nine but the square root of nine  is three and that equals 3x i can  multiply these together but what I  notice here is I could divide both sides  by 3 because I'm just trying to solve  for 1x the three is canceled and I can  see that x equals 2 25 now if you want  to check your answer you know you can  put 225 back in over here and I Lentz  and substitute over here and make sure  the left side in the right side equal  each other but that's probably how I  would approach this problem if you need  to review radicals go ahead and look at  these examples here again I'll see you  in the next lesson

